The BFF production dependency audit fails because its existing lockfile selects
proxy-addr 2.0.7, affected by
[GHSA-jqcg-44mw-7w3h](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-jqcg-44mw-7w3h).
Update that transitive dependency to the patched 2.0.8 and regenerate the owning
BFF npm dependency hash. Preserve the Express constraint, other dependency
versions and the existing loopback proxy setting. The frontend lock and hash
remain unchanged.

Both production dependency audits now report zero vulnerabilities. Verification
passed 57 BFF tests, 14 package/policy tests, 43 focused bootstrap/OAuth unit
tests, and the complete quality/type gate. The documentation audit checked 68
documents and 70 requirement IDs. Both immutable Nix packages and the
provenance, source-content and package-content checks passed at the exact
committed candidate. Their matching metadata reports the full revision with
dirty=false; the installed BFF contains proxy-addr 2.0.8.

This is a separate maintenance prerequisite for the network availability PR
#19; it contains no network feature changes. It implements the package integrity
and work-log requirements REQ-068/REQ-064. The
[session portal](https://vpsfree-cz.workspace.aitherdev.int.vpsfree.cz/2026-10-05-network-ipv4-left-counter/)
records exact revisions and verification evidence. The patch is unmerged and
undeployed; deployed exploitability has not been assessed.
